{"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Structure","modules":[{"name":"GraphProducts"}],"symbolKind":"struct","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"CartesianProduct"}],"remoteSource":{"fileName":"CartesianProduct.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct.swift#L49"},"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CartesianProduct","kind":"identifier"}],"externalID":"s:13GraphProducts16CartesianProductV","role":"symbol","title":"CartesianProduct"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts"]]},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The Cartesian product G □ H of two graphs, both directed or both undirected, as a lazy view."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Made by "},{"code":"cartesianProduct(_:_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CartesianProduct","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"First","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Second","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"overview","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Vertices","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" are the pairs ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Pair(g, h)"},{"text":". Vertex (g, h) has index ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(g)·|V(H)| + index(h)"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"row-major, the numbering of NamedGraphs’ grids, so ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertex(atIndex:)"},{"text":" costs one division and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertexIndex(of:)"},{"text":" one lookup in each factor.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Rows","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" list the G-moves first, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"(g′, h)"},{"text":" for each entry g′ of G’s row of g, then the H-moves","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"(g, h′)"},{"text":". They are computed from the factors’ index rows and never hash. A factor’s self-loop","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"gives a product self-loop, listed twice in an undirected row as the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Graph"},{"text":" law asks.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Edges"}]},{"type":"text","text":" are named by the factor edge and the factor vertex that generate them:"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".first(edge: e, vertexIndex: h)"},{"type":"text","text":" is G’s edge "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"type":"text","text":" at H’s vertex index "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"h"},{"type":"text","text":", and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".second(vertexIndex: g, edge: e)"},{"type":"text","text":" is H’s edge "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"type":"text","text":" at G’s vertex index "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"g"},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"type":"text","text":" lists every"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".first"},{"type":"text","text":" edge, G-edge outer, then every "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".second"},{"type":"text","text":" edge, G-vertex outer. When both factors have"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edge indices, so does the product: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(e)·|V(H)| + h"},{"type":"text","text":", then "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"m_G·|V(H)| + g·m_H + index(e)"},{"type":"text","text":"."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Products of multigraphs and pseudographs are defined edge by edge, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeCount"},{"type":"text","text":", the rows and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the degrees always agree."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To materialize it, pass it to a representation’s initializer. Products are "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Sendable"},{"type":"text","text":" when both"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"factors are."}]}]}],"sections":[],"relationshipsSections":[{"kind":"relationships","type":"conformsTo","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s9EscapableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s16SendableMetatypeP"],"title":"Conforms To"}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"topicSections":[{"generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/DirectedEdges","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/EdgeIndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/EdgeRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/IndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/UndirectedEdges"],"anchor":"Structures","title":"Structures"},{"generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/first","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/Car